 ## How to push data to vmagent
 
@@ -967,6 +967,42 @@ to all the metrics scraped by the given `vmagent` instance:
 
 See also [how to shard data among multiple remote storage systems](#sharding-among-remote-storages).
 
+## Limiting the number of targets per job
+
+Sometimes a scrape job backed by a service discovery mechanism (e.g. `kubernetes_sd_configs`, `consul_sd_configs`, `ec2_sd_configs`, etc.)
+can unexpectedly return a huge number of targets because of a misconfiguration on the discovery side
+(for example, a tag filter is accidentally removed, or a catalog query becomes too broad).
+Scraping all of these targets at once can spike CPU and memory usage on the `vmagent` instance,
+degrading scraping for every other job running on the same instance.
+
+The `max_scrape_targets` option at [scrape_configs](https://docs.victoriametrics.com/victoriametrics/sd_configs/#scrape_configs)
+protects against this by limiting the number of active scrape targets for the given `job_name`.
+The limit is applied uniformly no matter which service discovery mechanism(s) supplied the targets for the job.
+If the number of discovered targets for a job exceeds `max_scrape_targets`, then only up to `max_scrape_targets`
+of them are scraped, while the rest are excluded from scraping instead of being rejected or crashing `vmagent`.
+
+By default, `max_scrape_targets` is disabled (i.e. there is no limit on the number of targets per job).
+It can be set globally for all the jobs via the `max_scrape_targets` option in the `global` section
+of `-promscrape.config`, and overridden on a per-`job_name` basis:
+
+```yaml
+global:
+  max_scrape_targets: 1000
+
+scrape_configs:
+- job_name: foo
+  max_scrape_targets: 100
+  kubernetes_sd_configs:
+  - role: pod
+```
+
+Excluded targets are visible the same way as other dropped targets:
+* At the `http://vmagent:8429/targets` and `http://vmagent:8429/service-discovery` pages, where they are shown
+  with the `max_scrape_targets exceeded` drop reason.
+* At the `http://vmagent:8429/api/v1/targets` page, in the `droppedTargets` list.
+* Via the `vm_promscrape_scrape_pool_targets{scrape_job="<job_name>", status="excluded"}` metric,
+  which shows how many targets are currently excluded for the given `job_name` because of the cap.
+
 ## High availability
 
 It is possible to run multiple **identically configured** `vmagent` instances or `vmagent`
